{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","count":1,"questions":[{"id":51724,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>Consider the following two statements :</p>\n\n<p>I. if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> is a composite number, then n divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>!</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>II. There are infinitely many natural numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Then</p>","options":["I and II are true","I and II are false","I is true and II is false","I is false and II is true"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> is a composite number (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>)</p>\n\n<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></math> does not divide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>!</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ist</mi></math> statement is false</p>\n\n<p>(II) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> is such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is a prime number (Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math>) so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math> does not divide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></math> but there are infinite values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mfenced><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>104</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>109</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>114</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></mfenced></math> for which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math> divide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></math> but it is not true for every natural numbers.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da50d4844bc9ba015ec90"}]}